WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:07.310
We live in a small solar system in the
suburbs of the Milky Way galaxy.

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:16.720
Eight planets and more than 180 moons, all
orbiting the sun.

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:19.600
In our solar system.

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:22.530
The sun is the sheriff of the town.

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:25.570
We do whatever the sun wants us to do.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:34.790
The Sun's influence stretches more than one
light year in every direction and defines the

00:00:34.790 --> 00:00:36.590
boundary of our solar system.

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:42.020
Someplace out there is a place where the
sun's influence ends and the other star's

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:44.780
begins. That's the entrance to interstellar
space.

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:56.570
Interstellar space is the region between star
systems and our galaxy.

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:02.530
Until now, we've known little about it.

00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:07.810
For so long. We've thought of the space in
between the stars as entirely empty.

00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:09.910
But this turns out not to be true at all.

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:14.530
There's a lot going on out there, and it's
the forefront of astronomy.

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:20.900
One of the biggest clues about interstellar
space came right to our doorstep.

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:30.970
October 2017.

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:35.140
The Pan-starrs one telescope spots something
unusual.

00:01:36.610 --> 00:01:38.440
Pan-starrs is an observatory in.

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:43.630
Hawaii and it's scanning large sections of
the sky, looking for things that change.

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:51.310
All of a sudden there was this tiny little
visitor just screaming through the solar

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:54.010
system. It was going about 200,000 miles an
hour.

00:01:55.430 --> 00:02:00.920
It was a much faster object than what might
be expected for a solar system object.

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:05.630
And also its trajectory was such that it
seemed like its orbit was not bound to the

00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:06.200
sun.

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:14.510
It was totally unlike any other path, any
other trajectory, any other orbit in our

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:15.650
solar system.

00:02:17.110 --> 00:02:19.300
Astronomers reached an extraordinary
conclusion.

00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:27.850
It became very clear that, yeah, this was not
some solar system object falling from a long

00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:34.150
way away. This was something that came from
another star, and I think everybody was

00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:35.650
pretty amazed by that.

00:02:40.630 --> 00:02:46.300
Our first known interstellar visitor on a
flyby through our solar system.

00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:53.350
It was the very first object that we've ever
discovered that had originated outside the

00:02:53.350 --> 00:02:54.360
solar system.

00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:59.530
Everything else we've seen, every comet,
every asteroid originated within our solar

00:02:59.530 --> 00:03:00.160
system.

00:03:01.690 --> 00:03:06.160
Big mega solid object entering in our own
solar system.

00:03:06.170 --> 00:03:11.380
And that's something that, you know, I can
only dream of but had never thought it would

00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:12.610
actually be a reality.

00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:23.860
Scientists name the object Oumuamua Hawaiian
for a messenger from afar, arriving first.

00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:29.550
This thing came from interstellar space into
our solar system.

00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:32.310
And the main question is what could it be?

00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:40.010
The object's shape was mystifying.

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:44.000
It's almost sort of shaped like a cigar.

00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:46.530
It's ten times longer than it is wide.

00:03:47.330 --> 00:03:48.540
And this is extraordinary.

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:53.330
There's no object in our solar system that
we've ever measured that is this elongated.

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:02.670
Oumuamua looked so unnatural it sparked
scientists imaginations.

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:07.410
One of the things to remember about
scientists is that we're still human beings.

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:08.850
We have read science fiction.

00:04:08.850 --> 00:04:10.260
We have imaginations.

00:04:12.850 --> 00:04:16.810
I have to admit, when I first heard about it,
my first thought is that it was Rama from an

00:04:16.810 --> 00:04:18.850
Arthur C Clarke story, Rendezvous with Rama.

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:22.870
This is a very elongated spaceship that came
from another star.

00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:31.820
It reminded me of some designs we had for
interstellar spacecrafts that have to be much

00:04:31.820 --> 00:04:38.450
longer than they are wide in order to
minimize friction with the interstellar gas.

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:43.060
Could Oumuamua be an alien interstellar
spacecraft?

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:54.190
Astronomers, including Professor Avi Loeb,
took the idea seriously.

00:04:54.920 --> 00:05:00.620
We decided to follow this object using the
best telescopes in the world and observe

00:05:00.620 --> 00:05:02.900
whether there is any radio transmission from
it.

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:08.330
Astronomers hunted for signs of alien
communication.

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:19.630
But after eight hours of listening across
multiple frequencies, nothing.

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:24.290
Sadly, no emissions were detected.

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:26.780
It's almost certainly a natural object.

00:05:26.780 --> 00:05:28.840
I would bet all of my money on that.

00:05:28.850 --> 00:05:33.800
I was disappointed. I would have been much
more excited if we had found evidence for an

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:35.090
alien civilization.

00:05:38.890 --> 00:05:40.300
No little green men.

00:05:40.330 --> 00:05:44.740
This time a muumuu is a natural object.

00:05:45.310 --> 00:05:47.470
But what exactly is it?

00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:51.460
At first, observers thought it might be a
comet.

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:55.250
Is something that's mostly ice with a little
bit of rock.

00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:59.030
Those are the sorts of bodies that exist
really far out from the star and are the

00:05:59.030 --> 00:06:00.590
easiest things to eject.

00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:09.660
But a comet passing this close to the sun
would warm up, turning the ice into a gas,

00:06:09.690 --> 00:06:11.340
forming a vapor trail.

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:14.460
On a muamua.

00:06:14.490 --> 00:06:17.130
Astronomers saw no sign of this happening.

00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:23.490
There was no fuzziness around it that you
would expect from a comet as the ice was

00:06:23.490 --> 00:06:24.690
turned into a gas.

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:26.670
It was really mysterious.

00:06:26.670 --> 00:06:30.570
And so everybody who was observing it
thought it was an asteroid.

00:06:34.040 --> 00:06:36.920
As teams track the muumuu across the sky.

00:06:38.380 --> 00:06:40.300
There was an unexpected twist.

00:06:42.070 --> 00:06:47.590
As Oumuamua passed through our solar system,
it basically was falling in toward the sun,

00:06:47.590 --> 00:06:53.050
speeding up immensely as it passed the sun
before exiting the solar system in almost the

00:06:53.050 --> 00:06:54.100
opposite direction.

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:57.340
But then something weird happened as it was
moving away from the sun.

00:06:57.340 --> 00:07:02.260
It was slowing down as you'd expect, but it
wasn't slowing down fast enough.

00:07:03.780 --> 00:07:06.150
A muumuu gets a boost through our solar
system.

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:08.850
But how?

00:07:09.690 --> 00:07:11.670
So we think the reason is that it's
outgassing.

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:17.610
In other words, it was emitting a little bit
of gas and that was acting as a little bit of

00:07:17.610 --> 00:07:19.200
a rocket motor push on it.

00:07:19.590 --> 00:07:21.300
It's kind of like a little jet engine on the
surface.

00:07:22.050 --> 00:07:24.300
It just gives it a little thrust over time.

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:29.220
This tiny nudge reveals Oumuamua's true
identity.

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:34.710
So in that case, it is looking more like a
comet.

00:07:35.370 --> 00:07:38.760
It's just that that emission wasn't enough
to see.

00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:45.300
Powered by outgassing.

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:51.210
This interstellar nomad might travel from
star to star, but figuring out where in the

00:07:51.210 --> 00:07:54.960
galaxy it came from just got more difficult.

00:07:56.370 --> 00:08:01.350
Now that we know that Oumuamua has outgassed
which changes its velocity and changes its

00:08:01.350 --> 00:08:03.780
trajectory, it makes it much harder to do
that.

00:08:05.990 --> 00:08:12.860
A muumuu may now be safely racing away, but
the solar system faces bombardment from

00:08:12.860 --> 00:08:19.730
another interstellar source, firing at Earth
at close to the speed of light.

00:08:19.940 --> 00:08:27.800
Cosmic rays are the bullets of the universe,
and they are flooding interstellar space.

